---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import ReadingModes from "../../../components/ReadingModes.astro";
import Cite from "../../../components/Cite.astro";
import StorySources from "../../../components/StorySources.astro";
import StoryState from "../../../components/StoryState.astro";
import StateEvent from "../../../components/StateEvent.astro";
import DecisionTimeline from "../../../components/story/DecisionTimeline.astro";
import OutletCheck from "../../../components/story/OutletCheck.astro";
import QuoteCard from "../../../components/story/QuoteCard.astro";
import "../../../styles/story.css";
import { readPublishedState, eventPath } from "../../../lib/state.mjs";
import { affectedPeople, boardReview, boardVote, closureDispute, coverageChecks, coverageRecordCount, coverageVerdictCount, event, financialFiling, funding, laterCoverageRecordCount, timeline } from "../../../data/kennedy-center-2026-09-17-30-day-notice-before-demolition.mjs";
const state = readPublishedState().events.get("event-kennedy-center-2026-09-17-30-day-notice-before-demolition");
if (!state) throw new Error("Missing publication selection: event-kennedy-center-2026-09-17-30-day-notice-before-demolition");
if (state.status === "merged") return Astro.redirect(eventPath(state.survivor), 301);
---
{state.status === "retired" ? <StateEvent view={state} /> : <BaseLayout title={`${event.title} | The Catch`} current="Events">
  <div slot="locator" class="locator"><a href="/events/">Series</a><a href="/events/kennedy-center/">Kennedy Center</a><span>September 17, 2026</span></div>
  <div class="content">
    <main class="story" id="story-root">
      <p class="story-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Kennedy Center &middot; September 17, 2026</p>
      <h1 data-layer="fact">{event.title}</h1>
      <p class="story-dek" data-layer="fact">{event.dek}</p>
      <ReadingModes />

      <nav class="story-toc" aria-label="In this story" data-layer="fact">
        <details class="toc-disclosure" open><summary><span>In this story</span><span class="toc-current" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><ol>
          <li><a href="#what-happened">What happened</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-catch">The catch</a></li>
          <li><a href="#where-this-sits">Where this sits</a></li>
          <li><a href="#who-feels-it">Who feels it</a></li>
          <li><a href="#outlets">What the coverage got right, and what it got wrong</a></li>
          <li><a href="#what-happened-next">What happened next</a></li>
          <li><a href="#unknowns">What we do not know yet</a></li>
          <li><a href="#records">The records</a></li>
        </ol></details>
      </nav>

      <div class="kpi-strip" data-layer="fact">
        {event.kpis.map((k) => <div class="kpi"><span class="kpi-value">{k.value}<small>{k.unit ? ` ${k.unit}` : ""}</small></span><span class="kpi-label">{k.label}</span></div>)}
      </div>

      <aside class="three-things" data-layer="fact">
        <p class="tt-label">Three things to know</p>
        <ol>
          <li>Judge Christopher Cooper denied Representative Joyce Beatty's request for an emergency hearing on September 17. In the same minute order, he required written notice more than 30 days before the defendants implement a change to the project plans previously presented to the court, including demolition of the main building.<Cite s="upi-kennedy-center-2026-09-17" passage="more than 30 days before the implementation of any changes to the scope of the project plans presented to the Court" /></li>
          <li>Notice turns on changes to the scope of the project plans previously presented to the court.<Cite s="upi-kennedy-center-2026-09-17" passage="changes to the scope of the project plans presented to the Court" /></li>
          <li>The executive director said the immediate closure was a seven-day safety measure unless extended. The defendants characterized it as separate from the board's renovation decision.<Cite s="floca-declaration-86-1-2026-09-17" passage="which will last for seven days, unless extended" /><Cite s="defendants-response-86-2026-09-17" passage="separate from any Board decision related to closure for ongoing renovations" /></li>
        </ol>
      </aside>

      <div data-layer="proof" class="event-record-context">
        <p class="sources-line">This story rests on filings in Beatty v. Trump and D.C. Preservation League v. Trump; the court's May 29 opinions; the board's August meeting record; the August 28 joint report; the September 16 scheduling order; federal statutes governing the Kennedy Center; the September 17 minute order's operative sentence as reproduced by UPI and quoted in part by Beatty's counsel; and the coverage records checked against those documents.</p>
        <p class="sources-line">The September 17 minute-order text on the page comes from UPI's reproduction; Beatty's counsel quotes part of the same sentence.<Cite s="upi-kennedy-center-2026-09-17" passage="To avoid any confusion on that score given more recent events" /><Cite s="beatty-counsel-reaction-2026-09-17" passage="any changes to the scope of the project plans" /></p>
      </div>

      <section id="what-happened">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The request and the ruling</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">The judge refused the hearing and added an advance-notice rule</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Beatty asked for an emergency hearing after the main building closed and after the board voted to close it for renovation. The defendants answered that the immediate closure was separate from the renovation decision. Executive Director Matt Floca described it as a temporary safety measure and declared that structural deterioration created acute risks to public safety. He said the initial closure would last seven days unless extended.<Cite s="beatty-motion-84-2026-09-16" passage="MOTION FOR EMERGENCY HEARING" /><Cite s="defendants-response-86-2026-09-17" passage="separate from any Board decision related to closure for ongoing renovations" /><Cite s="floca-declaration-86-1-2026-09-17" passage="acute risks to public safety resulting from continued structural deterioration" /><Cite s="floca-declaration-86-1-2026-09-17" passage="which will last for seven days, unless extended" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The filing also put two public signals before Cooper. Trump had told reporters that without recognition for his administration's role in the renovation and fundraising, the building would close and end up being ripped down. The filing included a photograph of him reading a placard that appeared to say “Kennedy Center DEMOLISHED.” ABC reported that the published images did not show the entire placard and that the visible word was cut off at “DEMOLIS.” A Kennedy Center spokesperson separately said that nothing was being demolished.<Cite s="beatty-notice-85-2026-09-17" /><Cite s="beatty-notice-85-2026-09-17" passage="it will end up being ripped down" /><Cite s="beatty-notice-85-2026-09-17" passage="appears to read “Kennedy Center DEMOLISHED.”" /><Cite s="abc-kennedy-center-2026-09-17" passage="The published images don't show the entire placard" /><Cite s="abc-kennedy-center-2026-09-17" passage="cut off as “DEMOLIS.”" /><Cite s="cbs-kennedy-center-2026-09-17" passage="nothing is being demolished" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Cooper denied the hearing. He nevertheless ordered the defendants to give plaintiffs in both cases written notice before implementing a covered change.<Cite s="upi-kennedy-center-2026-09-17" passage="Beatty's request for a hearing was denied" /><Cite s="upi-kennedy-center-2026-09-17" passage="provide such notice in writing to Plaintiffs in both cases" /></p>
        <QuoteCard speaker="U.S. District Judge Christopher Cooper" when="September 17, 2026" where="Minute order, as reproduced by UPI; bracketed explanations are UPI's">
          <p>To avoid any confusion on that score given more recent events, the Court hereby ORDERS Defendants [the Kennedy Center board] to provide such notice in writing to Plaintiffs in both cases more than 30 days before the implementation of any changes to the scope of the project plans presented to the Court in D.C. Preservation League [a separate case], including but not limited to any 'demolition' of the Center's main building.<Cite s="upi-kennedy-center-2026-09-17" passage="To avoid any confusion on that score given more recent events" /></p>
        </QuoteCard>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The order also required the administration to update Cooper by September 23 on the status of the temporary closure and emergency repairs.<Cite s="abc-kennedy-center-2026-09-17" passage="update him on the status of the" /><Cite s="abc-kennedy-center-2026-09-17" passage="emergency repairs" /> Beatty's counsel said Cooper “ordered discovery into the Board’s actions related to a potential demolition.”<Cite s="beatty-counsel-reaction-2026-09-17" passage="ordered discovery into the Board’s actions related to a potential demolition" /> UPI published only an excerpt of the order; the “ordered discovery” wording is counsel's account.<Cite s="upi-kennedy-center-2026-09-17" passage="But he added another order" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="the-catch">
        <aside class="the-catch" data-layer="fact">
          <p class="tt-label">The catch</p>
          <ol>
            <li><h3>The period is more than 30 days.</h3>The operative sentence says notice must arrive more than 30 days before implementation.<Cite s="upi-kennedy-center-2026-09-17" passage="more than 30 days before the implementation" /></li>
            <li><h3>The order follows the plans already put before the court.</h3>It does not say “any major physical changes” require notice. That phrase comes from the AP headline. The trigger in the reproduced order is a change to the scope of the project plans presented in the preservation case, with demolition named as an example.<Cite s="pbs-ap-kennedy-center-2026-09-17" passage="any major physical changes" /><Cite s="upi-kennedy-center-2026-09-17" passage="changes to the scope of the project plans presented to the Court" /></li>
          </ol>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section id="where-this-sits">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The earlier record</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">The court had relied on a promise of no full demolition or rebuilding</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">In May, Cooper denied preliminary relief in the preservation case after considering sworn statements about the project. The opinion said the building's footprint and overall shape and size seemed unlikely to change permanently, and it described the anticipated open-space changes as relatively limited. The court said those findings were tentative and could change if the facts changed.<Cite s="preservation-opinion-45-2026-05-29" passage="footprint and overall shape and size seem unlikely to change in a permanent manner" /><Cite s="preservation-opinion-45-2026-05-29" passage="these relatively limited alterations" /><Cite s="preservation-opinion-45-2026-05-29" passage="tentative" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">In May, Cooper proceeded on the understanding that full demolition and a complete rebuilding were not in the cards.<Cite s="preservation-opinion-45-2026-05-29" passage="full demolition and a “Complete Rebuilding” are not in the cards" /> In September, his order required more than 30 days' notice before the defendants changed the scope of the project plans presented in that case, including demolition.<Cite s="upi-kennedy-center-2026-09-17" passage="more than 30 days before the implementation of any changes to the scope of the project plans presented to the Court" /> The May opinion said that if the Center were slated to be demolished and rebuilt, the League “might have a strong ultra vires claim” under the federal law requiring express congressional authority before a building is erected on federal public grounds in Washington.<Cite s="preservation-opinion-45-2026-05-29" passage="might have a strong ultra vires claim on its hands" /><Cite s="preservation-opinion-45-2026-05-29" passage="without express authority of Congress" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The opinion called it “far-fetched” to read the roughly {funding.appropriationLabel} appropriation for repair, restoration, the maintenance backlog and security structures as congressional approval of demolition and rebuilding.<Cite s="preservation-opinion-45-2026-05-29" passage="is far-fetched" /><Cite s="preservation-opinion-45-2026-05-29" passage="capital repair, restoration, maintenance backlog, and security structures" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">On August 24, the Justice Department argued that without the administration's work and recognition, the Center would deteriorate until it was “required to be taken down.” The filing said some people had proposed replacing it with a large outdoor amphitheater overlooking the Potomac River.<Cite s="defendants-response-recognition-68-2026-08-24" passage="will be required to be taken down" /><Cite s="defendants-response-recognition-68-2026-08-24" passage="a large outdoor amphitheater overlooking the Potomac River" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The parties' August 28 joint report made the relevant promise explicit: the defendants would inform the plaintiffs and the court if the board's plans reversed the position that the building would not be demolished and no new structure would be built.<Cite s="preservation-joint-report-62-2026-08-28" passage="Defendants have pledged to inform Plaintiffs and the Court" /><Cite s="preservation-joint-report-62-2026-08-28" passage="building will not be demolished and that no new structure will be constructed" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Congress appropriated about {funding.appropriationLabel} for capital repair, restoration, the maintenance backlog and security structures, with the money available through {funding.availabilityDeadline}.<Cite s="public-law-119-21-kennedy-center" passage="$256,657,000, to remain" /><Cite s="public-law-119-21-kennedy-center" passage="available until September 30, 2029" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The Atlantic reported that the new tax filing showed {financialFiling.totalRevenueLabel} in total revenue and a {financialFiling.badDebtExpenseLabel} bad-debt expense, that program-service revenue fell from {financialFiling.priorProgramRevenueLabel} to {financialFiling.currentProgramRevenueLabel}, and that the annual audit was unfinished.<Cite s="atlantic-kennedy-center-tax-return-2026-09-18" passage="more than $516 million in total revenue" /><Cite s="atlantic-kennedy-center-tax-return-2026-09-18" passage="$48 million “bad debt expense,”" /><Cite s="atlantic-kennedy-center-tax-return-2026-09-18" passage="Program-service revenue" /><Cite s="atlantic-kennedy-center-tax-return-2026-09-18" passage="the annual audit was not completed by the time of filing" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The August minutes say that keeping the main building partly open would leave the appropriation covering only about half of the work, with a {funding.partialOperationShortfallLabel} shortfall after utilities. The minutes put the closure estimate at about {funding.closureEstimateLabel}.<Cite s="board-minutes-68-3-2026-08-13" passage="would cover only approximately half of the work" /><Cite s="board-minutes-68-3-2026-08-13" passage="shortfall of $124M" /><Cite s="board-minutes-68-3-2026-08-13" passage="approximately $285 million" /></p>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact"><figcaption><strong>How the notice order arose</strong><span>The dated sequence in the court and board records.</span></figcaption><DecisionTimeline entries={timeline} /></figure>
      </section>

      <section id="who-feels-it">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">What changes in practice</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">The plaintiffs get warning; the parties dispute the immediate safety closure</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The reproduced order gives the plaintiffs in both cases a warning period before the defendants implement a covered project-scope change. It adds written notice to the earlier pledge to report a reversal of the position against full demolition or rebuilding.<Cite s="upi-kennedy-center-2026-09-17" passage="provide such notice in writing to Plaintiffs in both cases" /><Cite s="preservation-joint-report-62-2026-08-28" passage="change in the Board’s plans that would constitute a reversal" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The May opinion records Floca's estimate that between {affectedPeople.closureEstimateLow} and {affectedPeople.closureEstimateHigh} of roughly {affectedPeople.employeeEstimateTotal} employees would be affected by the closure. It also says several performance contracts would be affected, the Center had laid off at least {affectedPeople.layoffTranches} groups of staff including programming personnel, and {affectedPeople.remainingProgrammingStaff} programming staff remained.<Cite s="beatty-opinion-50-2026-05-29" passage="75 to 175 of roughly 300 employees at the Center would be affected" /><Cite s="beatty-opinion-50-2026-05-29" passage="The closure will affect several existing performance contracts" /><Cite s="beatty-opinion-50-2026-05-29" passage="laid off at least two tranches of staff members" /><Cite s="beatty-opinion-50-2026-05-29" passage="just ten members of the programming staff remain" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The opinion also identifies lost ticket and tourist revenue, trouble retaining audiences, the possible permanent loss of specialized arts professionals and long-term donor attrition among the downsides described in sworn testimony.<Cite s="beatty-opinion-50-2026-05-29" passage="lost ticket and tourist revenue; chronic challenges in audience retention" /><Cite s="beatty-opinion-50-2026-05-29" passage="potentially permanent loss of highly-specialized arts professionals; and long-term attrition of donors" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The August board plan keeps the REACH, the Center's expansion campus, open for educational and artistic programming, supports National Symphony Orchestra performances at off-site venues, and moves the Kennedy Center Honors and Mark Twain Prize off site while the main building is closed.<Cite s="beatty-opinion-50-2026-05-29" passage="expansion campus, known as the REACH" /><Cite s="board-minutes-68-3-2026-08-13" passage="shall continue to present educational and artistic programming at the REACH" /><Cite s="board-minutes-68-3-2026-08-13" passage="those performances offsite" /><Cite s="board-minutes-68-3-2026-08-13" passage="Kennedy Center Honors and the Mark Twain Prize for American Humor" /> The plan also moves the Kennedy memorial to the REACH and says it will remain open to the public.<Cite s="board-minutes-68-3-2026-08-13" passage="the Memorial to President John F. Kennedy be temporarily relocated to the REACH where it will remain open to the public" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">In a public statement, the {affectedPeople.preservationPlaintiffOrganizations} preservation plaintiffs said the court should consider a receiver or replacement leadership if the Center's leaders would not protect the building. They also named a limited receiver, independent monitor or other neutral officer. They framed those measures as steps for the court to consider.<Cite s="preservation-plaintiffs-statement-2026-09-17" passage="the court should consider appointing a receiver or replacing that leadership" /><Cite s="preservation-plaintiffs-statement-2026-09-17" passage="a receiver, limited receiver, independent monitor, or other neutral officer" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The defendants called the {closureDispute.closureDateLabel} safety closure separate from the renovation decision. Floca's staff email said the closure would let operations staff determine whether another severe safety incident was imminent and said it was not a decision to carry out the board's separate renovation-closure vote.<Cite s="floca-email-86-2-2026-09-16" passage="determine whether any other severe safety incident is imminent" /><Cite s="floca-email-86-2-2026-09-16" passage="this is not a decision to effectuate the Board's separate vote" /> Beatty's reply answered with a chronology: the building remained open for {closureDispute.daysOpenAfterIncident} days after the {closureDispute.incidentDateLabel} plaster fall, the Walter P Moore analysis dated from {closureDispute.assessmentMonthsLabel}, and the defendants identified no meaningfully new information between the incident and the closure. The closure came the day after the board vote.<Cite s="defendants-response-86-2026-09-17" passage="separate from any Board decision related to closure for ongoing renovations" /><Cite s="beatty-reply-87-2026-09-17" passage="kept the building open for twelve days after September 4" /><Cite s="beatty-reply-87-2026-09-17" passage="identified no meaningfully new information" /><Cite s="beatty-reply-87-2026-09-17" passage="was completed in June and July of 2026" /><Cite s="beatty-reply-87-2026-09-17" passage="immediately followed the Board’s closure vote" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Floca identified two immediate safety grounds: an engineering assessment of the roof-terrace canopy and the September 4 failure of a five-by-four-foot piece of ceiling plaster after water damage. The same declaration reported severe corrosion in 46 of 278 panels and said one-third of the panels required immediate replacement.<Cite s="floca-declaration-86-1-2026-09-17" passage="a roof terrace canopy assessment" /><Cite s="floca-declaration-86-1-2026-09-17" passage="failure of a five-by-four-foot piece of one-inch-thick ceiling plaster on September 4" /><Cite s="floca-declaration-86-1-2026-09-17" passage="severe corrosion in 46 panels" /><Cite s="floca-declaration-86-1-2026-09-17" passage="One-third of all panels require immediate replacement" /> Cooper denied the requested hearing. Neither UPI's reproduced order text nor counsel's release reports a resolution of the parties' factual dispute.<Cite s="upi-kennedy-center-2026-09-17" passage="Beatty's request for a hearing was denied" /><Cite s="beatty-counsel-reaction-2026-09-17" passage="Judge Cooper additionally ordered discovery" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Bridget Kennedy-Bailey, whose declaration supported Beatty's motion, said she saw fencing block the building and saw a forklift hit exterior pillars several times.<Cite s="kennedy-bailey-declaration-84-1-2026-09-16" passage="Bridget Kennedy-Bailey" /><Cite s="kennedy-bailey-declaration-84-1-2026-09-16" passage="forklift run into and hit the pillars" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The board's August minutes record {boardVote.votesCast} votes cast: {boardVote.inFavor} in favor and {boardVote.opposed} opposed. Another {boardVote.presentNotVoting} trustees were present but did not vote, and several were absent. The resolution directed closure of the main building for restoration and renovation and authorized legal steps to dissolve the injunction that prevented that closure.<Cite s="board-minutes-68-3-2026-08-13" passage="Motion carried by roll call, 23 in favor, 3 opposed" /><Cite s="board-minutes-68-3-2026-08-13" passage="Present, not voting" /><Cite s="board-minutes-68-3-2026-08-13" passage="seeking to dissolve the preliminary injunction" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">That vote came with acknowledged gaps. Beatty said she received {boardReview.slidesReceived} slides {boardReview.daysBeforeMeeting} days before the meeting, Whitehouse listed {boardReview.requestedAnalyses} baseline requirements, and Larsen said the board lacked the full {boardReview.missingComparisonYearsLabel} analysis. The defendants later said the full Delta report went to trustees on {boardReview.deltaDistributionDateLabel}.<Cite s="board-minutes-68-3-2026-08-13" passage="only two PowerPoint slides, received two days prior" /><Cite s="board-minutes-68-3-2026-08-13" passage="summarizing his request in six points" /><Cite s="board-minutes-68-3-2026-08-13" passage="characterized these as baseline requirements" /><Cite s="board-minutes-68-3-2026-08-13" passage="did not have the full four-year analysis" /><Cite s="defendants-statement-facts-89-2-2026-09-18" passage="On August 31, the Center’s management distributed the finalized Delta Consulting" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="outlets">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Cross-source check</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What the coverage got right, and what it got wrong</h2>
        <p data-layer="fact">The notice-order check uses {coverageRecordCount} coverage records. {coverageVerdictCount} receive verdict cards below. ABC supplied the status-report detail and the placard limitation; CBS carried the Center's no-demolition response; and the earlier USA Today report carried Trump's ripped-down remark, which was checked against the filing that put it before the court. Another {laterCoverageRecordCount} AP reports document the next-day protest and Trump's response to a demolition question.<Cite s="abc-kennedy-center-2026-09-17" passage="update him on the status" /><Cite s="abc-kennedy-center-2026-09-17" passage="The published images don't show the entire placard" /><Cite s="cbs-kennedy-center-2026-09-17" passage="nothing is being demolished" /><Cite s="usa-today-kennedy-center-comments-2026-09-16" passage="end up being ripped down" /><Cite s="beatty-notice-85-2026-09-17" passage="it will end up being ripped down" /><Cite s="ap-kennedy-center-protest-2026-09-18" passage="Thousands of people joined outside" /><Cite s="ap-trump-kennedy-center-2026-09-18" passage="did not directly respond when a reporter asked whether he planned to demolish the theater" /></p>
        <div data-layer="fact">
          {coverageChecks.map((item) => <OutletCheck outlet={item.outlet} claim={item.claim} verdict={item.verdict} chip={item.chip} status={item.status}><Cite s={item.source} passage={item.passage} />{item.secondPassage && <Cite s={item.source} passage={item.secondPassage} />}{item.proposalSource && <Cite s={item.proposalSource} passage={item.proposalPassage} />}{item.recordSource && <Cite s={item.recordSource} passage={item.recordPassage} />}</OutletCheck>)}
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="what-happened-next">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The next dated steps</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">The closure and renovation claims moved onto separate tracks</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The existing scheduling order had already opened expedited discovery on Beatty's closure claim. It set September 23 deadlines for written discovery requests and deposition notices, September 30 for written responses, October 7 for completed depositions and October 9 for the close of discovery.<Cite s="scheduling-order-82-2026-09-16" passage="Requests for Written and Document Discovery" /><Cite s="scheduling-order-82-2026-09-16" passage="All Discovery Closed" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The September 17 order added the update and advance-notice requirements. The September 16 scheduling order separately set deadlines for a motion to vacate the preliminary injunction and for discovery on the closure claim.<Cite s="beatty-counsel-reaction-2026-09-17" passage="required the Board to update the Court by September 23" /><Cite s="upi-kennedy-center-2026-09-17" passage="more than 30 days before the implementation" /><Cite s="scheduling-order-82-2026-09-16" passage="Motion to Vacate the Preliminary" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">On September 18, the defendants filed a motion to dissolve the May preliminary injunction and a renewed motion for partial summary judgment on Beatty's remaining closure and construction claims.<Cite s="defendants-motion-vacate-88-2026-09-18" passage="DEFENDANTS’ MOTION TO DISSOLVE PRELIMINARY INJUNCTION" /><Cite s="defendants-summary-judgment-89-2026-09-18" passage="DEFENDANTS’ RENEWED MOTION FOR" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Floca says management distributed a packet on {boardReview.managementPacketDateLabel} for discussion at the {boardReview.reconsiderationDateLabel} board meeting and attached it to his declaration as Exhibit B. The declaration dates the packet to the closure deliberations but does not reproduce its contents or identify the recognition proposal.<Cite s="floca-declaration-89-3-2026-09-18" passage="On September 13, 2026, the Center distributed to trustees a packet of materials" /><Cite s="floca-declaration-89-3-2026-09-18" passage="attached to this declaration as Exhibit B" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The defense filings agree that the board voted again to close the main building, but they quote the timing differently. The statement of facts says it would close to patrons immediately; the supporting memorandum says closure would follow dissolution of the preliminary injunction.<Cite s="defendants-statement-facts-89-2-2026-09-18" passage="shall be closed to patrons forthwith" /><Cite s="defendants-consolidated-memorandum-88-1-2026-09-18" passage="forthwith upon the dissolution of the preliminary injunction" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The defendants argued that phased closure would create a {funding.phasedClosureClaimLabel} funding shortfall, compared with less than {funding.fullClosureShortfallClaimLabel} for full closure. These were the defendants' assertions. Floca's declaration identifies an earlier JLL plan and Delta presentation as filed under seal. It says the finalized Delta report and certain appendices were attached as Exhibit A, while appendices with sensitive architectural drawings were excluded.<Cite s="defendants-consolidated-memorandum-88-1-2026-09-18" passage="a $300 million funding shortfall" /><Cite s="floca-declaration-89-3-2026-09-18" passage="appears under seal on the docket" /><Cite s="floca-declaration-89-3-2026-09-18" passage="correct copy of that report and certain of its appendices is attached to this declaration as" /><Cite s="floca-declaration-89-3-2026-09-18" passage="appendices that contain sensitive architectural drawings" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Later on September 18, thousands joined the Hands Around the Kennedy Center protest, and some formed a human chain around the building. The organizer invited artists, patrons, staff members, union members and volunteers.<Cite s="ap-kennedy-center-protest-2026-09-18" passage="Thousands of people joined outside" /><Cite s="ap-kennedy-center-protest-2026-09-18" passage="formed a human chain around the center" /><Cite s="action-network-hands-around-kennedy-center-2026-09-18" passage="Worked at the Center as a staff member, union member, or volunteer" /> AP also reported that Duke Ellington students had lost access to Washington National Opera costumes and Millennium Stage performances.<Cite s="ap-kennedy-center-protest-2026-09-18" passage="borrowed the beautiful costumes and the beautiful dresses from the Washington National Opera" /><Cite s="ap-kennedy-center-protest-2026-09-18" passage="performed on the center’s Millennium Stage" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="unknowns">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Open questions</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What we do not know yet</h2>
        <ul data-layer="fact">
          <li data-absence>As of September 19, the docket entries through that date in both Kennedy Center cases and the coverage saved for this story contain no approved change to the project's scope or demolition plan. Floca's declaration says the earlier JLL report and Delta presentation remained under seal. It also says the finalized Delta report and some appendices were filed as Exhibit A.</li>
          <li data-absence>As of September 19, the two defense filings do not establish which closure condition appears in the September 15 preliminary minutes; the minutes themselves are not among the records. The two defense filings quote the resolution differently.</li>
          <li>Trump's intent remained unresolved on September 18. The Associated Press reported that he did not directly respond when asked whether he planned to demolish the theater.<Cite s="ap-trump-kennedy-center-2026-09-18" passage="did not directly respond when a reporter asked whether he planned to demolish the theater" /></li>
        </ul>
      </section>

